1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:03,483
Some SAT questions don't
ask what a passage says.

2
00:00:03,503 --> 00:00:05,234
They ask what it's doing.

3
00:00:05,254 --> 00:00:08,805
Which choice best states the
main purpose of the text?

4
00:00:08,825 --> 00:00:11,912
Which choice best describes
its overall structure?

5
00:00:11,932 --> 00:00:14,406
What's the function of
the underlined sentence?

6
00:00:14,426 --> 00:00:17,798
The most common mistake is picking a
choice that's true about the passage,

7
00:00:17,818 --> 00:00:19,855
but only covers one part of it.

8
00:00:20,069 --> 00:00:25,656
In this video: a three-step method,
six original SAT-style questions,

9
00:00:25,676 --> 00:00:29,355
and the four traps these
questions use again and again.

10
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:32,146
Every sentence in a
passage does two things.

11
00:00:32,166 --> 00:00:35,095
It says something, and it does a job.

12
00:00:35,166 --> 00:00:37,988
On these questions, the
job is what matters.

13
00:00:38,047 --> 00:00:39,776
Here are the jobs you'll see most.

14
00:00:39,796 --> 00:00:43,011
A claim is the point the
author wants you to accept.

15
00:00:43,119 --> 00:00:44,619
Evidence supports it.

16
00:00:44,654 --> 00:00:46,689
An example shows one case of it.

17
00:00:46,726 --> 00:00:49,240
A contrast pushes
against what came before.

18
00:00:49,260 --> 00:00:51,741
A concession admits a
point on the other side.

19
00:00:51,761 --> 00:00:54,011
And a conclusion wraps things up.

20
00:00:54,503 --> 00:00:56,982
These questions belong
to Craft and Structure,

21
00:00:57,002 --> 00:01:00,304
about twenty-eight percent of
the Reading and Writing section.

22
00:01:00,324 --> 00:01:02,753
That's the largest of the four domains.

23
00:01:02,818 --> 00:01:04,370
You'll see three versions.

24
00:01:04,390 --> 00:01:08,568
Main purpose asks why the
author wrote the whole text.

25
00:01:08,604 --> 00:01:11,512
Overall structure asks
for the jobs in order,

26
00:01:11,532 --> 00:01:12,941
from start to finish.

27
00:01:12,961 --> 00:01:18,243
And function asks what one underlined
sentence does for the text as a whole.

28
00:01:18,263 --> 00:01:19,136
Notice the scope.

29
00:01:19,156 --> 00:01:22,386
Purpose and structure
cover the whole passage.

30
00:01:22,406 --> 00:01:27,029
Function covers one sentence, but
always in relation to the rest.

31
00:01:27,049 --> 00:01:29,528
A choice with the wrong scope is wrong,

32
00:01:29,548 --> 00:01:32,333
even if every word in it is true.

33
00:01:32,380 --> 00:01:36,718
Step one: label every sentence's
job before you look at the choices.

34
00:01:36,738 --> 00:01:39,788
Claim, evidence, contrast, whatever it is.

35
00:01:39,808 --> 00:01:42,660
A one-word note in your head is enough.

36
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:45,339
Step two: predict the
answer in your own words,

37
00:01:45,359 --> 00:01:47,017
starting with a verb.

38
00:01:47,037 --> 00:01:48,180
Explains.

39
00:01:48,323 --> 00:01:49,267
Argues.

40
00:01:49,287 --> 00:01:50,446
Describes.

41
00:01:50,466 --> 00:01:51,644
Challenges.

42
00:01:52,179 --> 00:01:55,495
On a structure question, string
your labels together in order.

43
00:01:55,515 --> 00:01:58,745
Step three: check each choice three ways.

44
00:01:58,765 --> 00:01:59,781
Is the verb right?

45
00:01:59,801 --> 00:02:01,638
Does it cover the right
amount of the text?

46
00:02:01,658 --> 00:02:03,959
And are the parts in the right order?

47
00:02:03,979 --> 00:02:05,837
Miss one, and it's out.

48
00:02:05,911 --> 00:02:07,090
Question one.

49
00:02:07,233 --> 00:02:10,054
Flamingos get their pink
color from their food.

50
00:02:10,090 --> 00:02:14,856
The algae and small crustaceans they
eat in shallow lakes and lagoons contain

51
00:02:14,876 --> 00:02:16,890
pigments called carotenoids.

52
00:02:16,910 --> 00:02:21,713
As a flamingo digests this food, its
body extracts the pigments and deposits

53
00:02:21,733 --> 00:02:24,115
them in its new feathers as they grow.

54
00:02:24,135 --> 00:02:28,365
For this reason, zoos add
carotenoids to their flamingos' food.

55
00:02:28,385 --> 00:02:32,849
Without these pigments, a flamingo's
new feathers would grow in much paler.

56
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:36,807
Which choice best states the
main purpose of the text?

57
00:02:36,827 --> 00:02:38,006
Pause and try it.

58
00:02:41,197 --> 00:02:42,197
Label it.

59
00:02:42,375 --> 00:02:44,982
Sentence one is the claim:
the color comes from food.

60
00:02:45,089 --> 00:02:47,697
Sentences two and three
explain how that works.

61
00:02:47,875 --> 00:02:50,641
The last sentence shows
what it means in practice,

62
00:02:50,661 --> 00:02:51,732
in zoos.

63
00:02:51,875 --> 00:02:56,427
Prediction: the text explains how
a flamingo's diet makes it pink.

64
00:02:56,447 --> 00:02:57,661
That's choice C.

65
00:02:57,688 --> 00:02:59,116
Now the traps.

66
00:02:59,331 --> 00:03:02,775
A, the lakes where
flamingos feed, is true,

67
00:03:02,795 --> 00:03:03,989
but it's one detail.

68
00:03:04,009 --> 00:03:05,402
That's too narrow.

69
00:03:05,545 --> 00:03:08,738
D says many birds get
their colors from food.

70
00:03:08,758 --> 00:03:10,310
That's too broad.

71
00:03:10,330 --> 00:03:12,724
The text is only about flamingos.

72
00:03:12,745 --> 00:03:17,923
And B says the text argues that zoos
should change what they feed flamingos.

73
00:03:17,959 --> 00:03:20,224
The text never takes a side.

74
00:03:20,244 --> 00:03:21,458
It explains.

75
00:03:21,672 --> 00:03:24,280
When the verb is wrong,
the choice is wrong.

76
00:03:24,359 --> 00:03:25,537
Question two.

77
00:03:25,823 --> 00:03:30,946
In the early eighteen hundreds, French
army officer Charles Barbier devised a

78
00:03:30,966 --> 00:03:34,553
code of raised dots that
could be read by touch.

79
00:03:34,573 --> 00:03:39,716
But the code had drawbacks: its symbols
stood for sounds rather than letters,

80
00:03:39,859 --> 00:03:43,145
and each was too tall to
feel with one fingertip.

81
00:03:43,217 --> 00:03:46,589
Louis Braille, a student at a
Paris school for blind children,

82
00:03:46,609 --> 00:03:49,233
kept the raised dots but used smaller,

83
00:03:49,253 --> 00:03:51,697
six-dot cells that stood for letters,

84
00:03:51,717 --> 00:03:54,503
making the code far easier to read.

85
00:03:54,753 --> 00:03:58,610
Which choice best describes the
overall structure of the text?

86
00:03:58,651 --> 00:03:59,830
Pause and try it.

87
00:04:04,020 --> 00:04:05,358
Label each sentence.

88
00:04:05,378 --> 00:04:07,143
One: an early system.

89
00:04:07,163 --> 00:04:08,572
Two: its problems.

90
00:04:08,592 --> 00:04:12,286
Three: a later system that keeps the
good part and fixes the problems.

91
00:04:12,306 --> 00:04:15,628
String those together, in order,
and that's your prediction.

92
00:04:15,674 --> 00:04:18,153
Choice B: it introduces an early system,

93
00:04:18,173 --> 00:04:22,119
explains its drawbacks, and then
describes how a later system built on that

94
00:04:22,139 --> 00:04:24,476
system while avoiding those drawbacks.

95
00:04:24,496 --> 00:04:27,047
Every part matches, in the right order.

96
00:04:27,067 --> 00:04:28,478
B is the answer.

97
00:04:28,498 --> 00:04:30,121
A flips the order.

98
00:04:30,141 --> 00:04:34,034
It says the student came first and
the officer revised his work later.

99
00:04:34,176 --> 00:04:36,120
Right people, wrong order.

100
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:37,335
That's the reversed trap.

101
00:04:37,355 --> 00:04:40,906
C says the text argues
something it never argues,

102
00:04:40,926 --> 00:04:44,212
and D ends on the school,
which is just a detail.

103
00:04:44,259 --> 00:04:45,168
Question three.

104
00:04:45,188 --> 00:04:47,688
Here the second sentence is underlined.

105
00:04:47,723 --> 00:04:51,881
Crows appear able to recognize
individual human faces.

106
00:04:51,901 --> 00:04:56,989
In one study, researchers wearing a
particular rubber mask trapped several wild

107
00:04:57,009 --> 00:04:59,474
crows and released them unharmed.

108
00:04:59,546 --> 00:05:04,419
For at least two years afterward, crows
in the area scolded people wearing that

109
00:05:04,439 --> 00:05:07,096
mask but ignored people
wearing a different one.

110
00:05:07,116 --> 00:05:10,453
Many of the scolding crows had
never been trapped themselves,

111
00:05:10,473 --> 00:05:15,061
which suggests that crows can learn
from one another which faces to treat as

112
00:05:15,081 --> 00:05:15,987
threats.

113
00:05:16,007 --> 00:05:20,773
Which choice best describes the function
of the underlined sentence in the text

114
00:05:20,793 --> 00:05:21,761
as a whole?

115
00:05:21,781 --> 00:05:22,960
Pause and try it.

116
00:05:27,151 --> 00:05:28,758
Label the jobs.

117
00:05:29,079 --> 00:05:33,258
Sentence one is the claim:
crows can recognize faces.

118
00:05:33,508 --> 00:05:36,702
The underlined sentence describes
a study that backs it up.

119
00:05:36,722 --> 00:05:37,865
That's evidence.

120
00:05:37,972 --> 00:05:40,345
The last sentence adds a further finding:

121
00:05:40,365 --> 00:05:42,801
crows learn from each other.

122
00:05:42,821 --> 00:05:47,766
So the underlined sentence gives evidence
for the claim in the first sentence.

123
00:05:47,786 --> 00:05:48,959
That's choice D.

124
00:05:48,979 --> 00:05:51,530
The trap is A: it states the main claim.

125
00:05:51,550 --> 00:05:53,209
That's a real job in this passage.

126
00:05:53,229 --> 00:05:55,031
It just belongs to the first sentence,

127
00:05:55,051 --> 00:05:56,459
not the underlined one.

128
00:05:56,479 --> 00:06:00,587
On function questions, make sure the job
matches the sentence they asked about.

129
00:06:00,623 --> 00:06:03,353
B says the last sentence
calls the study into question.

130
00:06:03,373 --> 00:06:03,923
It doesn't.

131
00:06:03,943 --> 00:06:05,194
It builds on it.

132
00:06:05,408 --> 00:06:09,122
And C invents a debate that
never appears in the text.

133
00:06:09,149 --> 00:06:10,343
Those are the four traps.

134
00:06:10,363 --> 00:06:13,164
Too narrow: it's true,
but it covers one part.

135
00:06:13,184 --> 00:06:16,113
Too broad: it covers
more than the text does.

136
00:06:16,149 --> 00:06:18,914
Wrong verb: right topic, but
the text doesn't do that.

137
00:06:18,934 --> 00:06:21,613
And reversed: the right
parts in the wrong order.

138
00:06:21,685 --> 00:06:24,628
The next three are harder:
a passage from a story,

139
00:06:24,648 --> 00:06:27,022
a sentence that admits a
point to the other side,

140
00:06:27,042 --> 00:06:30,470
and a structure question where
three choices start out the same.

141
00:06:30,544 --> 00:06:32,687
Question four is literary.

142
00:06:32,759 --> 00:06:37,024
The following text is from an original
short story written for this video.

143
00:06:37,044 --> 00:06:41,024
Teo has reluctantly agreed to spend
the summer helping at his grandmother's

144
00:06:41,044 --> 00:06:41,853
bakery.

145
00:06:41,873 --> 00:06:44,852
Teo had expected the four o'clock
mornings to be the worst part,

146
00:06:44,872 --> 00:06:49,389
and for the first week they were: he moved
through the dark kitchen half asleep,

147
00:06:49,409 --> 00:06:52,173
measuring flour wherever
his grandmother pointed.

148
00:06:52,193 --> 00:06:56,302
Then, on the second Monday, she
let him shape the loaves himself.

149
00:06:56,347 --> 00:07:00,398
When the day's first customer lifted
one from the shelf and held it close to

150
00:07:00,418 --> 00:07:05,005
breathe in its warmth, Teo realized he
had stopped counting the hours until

151
00:07:05,025 --> 00:07:05,934
noon.

152
00:07:05,954 --> 00:07:09,451
Which choice best states the
main purpose of the text?

153
00:07:09,471 --> 00:07:10,650
Pause and try it.

154
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:17,035
In a story, the jobs
are a little different.

155
00:07:17,055 --> 00:07:19,177
Track where a character's feelings start,

156
00:07:19,197 --> 00:07:21,356
what happens, and where they end up.

157
00:07:21,376 --> 00:07:23,570
Teo starts out dreading the work.

158
00:07:23,590 --> 00:07:25,249
He gets to shape the loaves.

159
00:07:25,269 --> 00:07:27,447
Then he watches a customer enjoy one.

160
00:07:27,502 --> 00:07:28,696
And he's stopped counting the hours.

161
00:07:28,716 --> 00:07:31,303
He isn't waiting for the
shift to end anymore.

162
00:07:31,323 --> 00:07:35,874
Prediction: a character starts to
enjoy work he expected to dislike.

163
00:07:35,894 --> 00:07:37,037
That's choice A.

164
00:07:37,088 --> 00:07:38,568
C is the reversed trap.

165
00:07:38,588 --> 00:07:43,373
It runs the change backward, from enjoying
the work to being frustrated by it.

166
00:07:43,445 --> 00:07:46,675
B, the customer's reaction, is too narrow.

167
00:07:46,695 --> 00:07:48,496
It's one moment, not the point.

168
00:07:48,516 --> 00:07:51,031
And D, why the grandmother needed help,

169
00:07:51,051 --> 00:07:52,228
never comes up.

170
00:07:52,248 --> 00:07:53,228
Question five.

171
00:07:53,248 --> 00:07:55,300
The second sentence is underlined.

172
00:07:55,320 --> 00:07:59,942
Daylight saving time was adopted in many
countries partly to conserve energy.

173
00:07:59,962 --> 00:08:04,300
Moving clocks forward does reduce how
much electricity people use for lighting

174
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:05,304
in the evening.

175
00:08:05,324 --> 00:08:08,982
But when economists studied Indiana,
where most counties began observing

176
00:08:09,002 --> 00:08:11,911
daylight saving time
only in two thousand six,

177
00:08:11,931 --> 00:08:16,473
they found that household electricity
use rose after the change.

178
00:08:16,493 --> 00:08:20,580
More heating on dark mornings and more
air conditioning on warm evenings,

179
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:23,979
they concluded, outweighed
the savings on lighting.

180
00:08:23,999 --> 00:08:28,764
Which choice best describes the function
of the underlined sentence in the text

181
00:08:28,784 --> 00:08:29,752
as a whole?

182
00:08:29,772 --> 00:08:30,951
Pause and try it.

183
00:08:35,142 --> 00:08:39,122
On its own, the underlined sentence
sounds like support for daylight saving

184
00:08:39,142 --> 00:08:39,979
time.

185
00:08:39,999 --> 00:08:43,213
But look at what comes next: the word but,

186
00:08:43,319 --> 00:08:46,785
and a finding that total
electricity use went up.

187
00:08:46,815 --> 00:08:48,937
So the sentence admits a real benefit,

188
00:08:48,957 --> 00:08:51,938
and then the text shows
that benefit is outweighed.

189
00:08:51,958 --> 00:08:53,152
That's a concession.

190
00:08:53,172 --> 00:08:57,616
Words like does, admittedly, and
it's true that often signal one.

191
00:08:57,636 --> 00:08:58,993
The answer is C.

192
00:08:59,051 --> 00:09:00,317
B is the trap.

193
00:09:00,337 --> 00:09:04,245
It says the sentence shows that daylight
saving time lowers total household

194
00:09:04,265 --> 00:09:05,602
electricity use.

195
00:09:05,622 --> 00:09:10,459
The sentence is only about lighting,
and the study found total use went up.

196
00:09:10,479 --> 00:09:14,388
A calls it the study's main
finding, and D calls it a drawback.

197
00:09:14,408 --> 00:09:15,358
It's a benefit.

198
00:09:15,378 --> 00:09:16,322
Last one.

199
00:09:16,342 --> 00:09:17,414
The hardest.

200
00:09:17,699 --> 00:09:22,144
In eighteen forty-six, astronomers
discovered Neptune after calculating that the

201
00:09:22,164 --> 00:09:27,107
gravity of an unseen planet could explain
why Uranus strayed from its predicted

202
00:09:27,127 --> 00:09:27,847
path.

203
00:09:27,867 --> 00:09:31,668
When Mercury's orbit was also found
to differ slightly from predictions,

204
00:09:31,688 --> 00:09:35,525
some astronomers applied the same
reasoning and searched for a planet even

205
00:09:35,545 --> 00:09:38,688
closer to the Sun, which
they called Vulcan.

206
00:09:38,795 --> 00:09:40,724
None was ever found.

207
00:09:40,765 --> 00:09:45,352
In nineteen fifteen, Albert Einstein's
general theory of relativity,

208
00:09:45,372 --> 00:09:50,030
a new account of gravity, explained
Mercury's orbit without any additional

209
00:09:50,050 --> 00:09:50,959
planet.

210
00:09:50,979 --> 00:09:55,194
Which choice best describes the
overall structure of the text?

211
00:09:55,237 --> 00:09:56,324
Pause and try it.

212
00:09:56,344 --> 00:09:57,915
Take a little longer on this one.

213
00:10:02,130 --> 00:10:03,003
Label it.

214
00:10:03,023 --> 00:10:05,217
Sentence one: a line of
reasoning that worked.

215
00:10:05,237 --> 00:10:07,166
It found Neptune.

216
00:10:07,273 --> 00:10:10,486
Sentence two: the same
reasoning, tried on a new puzzle.

217
00:10:10,630 --> 00:10:12,430
Sentence three: it failed.

218
00:10:12,450 --> 00:10:15,303
Sentence four: what
finally solved the puzzle.

219
00:10:15,323 --> 00:10:16,787
Now look at the choices.

220
00:10:16,894 --> 00:10:19,716
A, B, and C all start the same way.

221
00:10:19,751 --> 00:10:22,731
That's how hard structure
questions hide the trap:

222
00:10:22,751 --> 00:10:25,874
the first part is right,
and the mistake comes later.

223
00:10:25,894 --> 00:10:27,746
So check every part.

224
00:10:27,766 --> 00:10:30,496
A says the reasoning led
to a second discovery.

225
00:10:30,516 --> 00:10:32,246
But Vulcan was never found.

226
00:10:32,266 --> 00:10:35,889
C gets two parts right, then says the
text argues that astronomers should have

227
00:10:35,909 --> 00:10:37,015
given up sooner.

228
00:10:37,193 --> 00:10:38,623
It never argues that.

229
00:10:38,730 --> 00:10:41,051
That's the wrong verb
trap, hiding at the end.

230
00:10:41,103 --> 00:10:42,531
D is reversed.

231
00:10:42,567 --> 00:10:45,690
Neptune came first, not
after the failed search.

232
00:10:45,710 --> 00:10:48,332
B matches every part, in order.

233
00:10:48,352 --> 00:10:49,996
The answer is B.

234
00:10:50,016 --> 00:10:50,675
Here's your routine.

235
00:10:50,695 --> 00:10:53,639
Read the question first,
so you know the scope:

236
00:10:53,659 --> 00:10:56,124
the whole text, or one sentence.

237
00:10:56,231 --> 00:11:00,230
Then read the passage and label
each sentence's job as you go.

238
00:11:00,269 --> 00:11:03,212
Predict with a verb before
you look at the choices.

239
00:11:03,232 --> 00:11:05,713
Then check every choice
for the right verb,

240
00:11:05,733 --> 00:11:07,819
the right scope, and the right order.

241
00:11:07,839 --> 00:11:12,376
On long choices, check every part,
because the mistake is often near the end.

242
00:11:12,438 --> 00:11:14,989
Signal words do half the labeling for you.

243
00:11:15,009 --> 00:11:18,403
But, however, and yet
usually start a contrast.

244
00:11:18,581 --> 00:11:22,347
Admittedly, or it's true that,
usually means a concession.

245
00:11:22,367 --> 00:11:24,617
For example means an example.

246
00:11:24,659 --> 00:11:28,710
You have a little over a minute
per Reading and Writing question,

247
00:11:28,730 --> 00:11:29,873
on average.

248
00:11:30,052 --> 00:11:35,134
Labeling takes a few seconds, and it saves
you from rereading the whole passage.

249
00:11:35,154 --> 00:11:35,920
Recap.

250
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:37,098
Label the jobs.

251
00:11:37,118 --> 00:11:38,706
Predict with a verb.

252
00:11:38,726 --> 00:11:41,277
Then check the verb, the
scope, and the order,

253
00:11:41,297 --> 00:11:44,490
and cross out anything
too narrow, too broad,

254
00:11:44,510 --> 00:11:46,654
with the wrong verb, or reversed.

255
00:11:46,714 --> 00:11:50,372
Every question in this video
was written for this video,

256
00:11:50,392 --> 00:11:52,836
in the style of the real test.

257
00:11:52,856 --> 00:11:57,086
For official practice, the College Board's
free Question Bank lets you filter by

258
00:11:57,106 --> 00:12:00,034
skill, including Text
Structure and Purpose.

259
00:12:00,082 --> 00:12:01,689
If this helped, subscribe.

260
00:12:02,011 --> 00:12:05,884
Next up: Cross-Text Connections,
the questions with two passages,

261
00:12:05,904 --> 00:12:08,297
